"The word 'Tabāraka' expresses the exaltation of Allah in His essence and attributes, and the permanence of His goodness and blessing. This word derives from the root 'barakah' and describes the continuity and increase of divine good, much like water gathering and rising in a pool. When used regarding Allah Almighty (Allah Ta'ala), it must be understood as transcending all doubts of createdness or alteration. Exegetes have interpreted this word in two primary senses: first, that His existence is eternal without beginning and without end; and second, that His goodness and favor abound. By combining these two meanings, it is expressed that Allah is the highest and most consummate in His essence, attributes, and actions.\n\nThe word 'Furqan' derives from the roots 'farq' and 'tafriq', meaning to distinguish, to separate truth from falsehood. Furqan, which is one of the names of the Quran, emphasizes that it is a miraculous and inimitable book that distinguishes between truth and falsehood. Along with the expression 'Tabāraka' mentioned in the verse, it is stated how exalted and blessed is Allah, who sent down this Furqan. The purpose of sending down the Furqan is to be a warner to all worlds; that is, to warn people and caution them against Allah's punishment. This warning also contains good tidings and mercy for the believers.\n\nAllah Almighty possesses the sovereignty of the heavens and the earth; He has neither partner nor offspring. He created everything and determined for it a precise measure. For this reason, nothing created can be a partner to Him. Despite this, the disbelievers have taken as deities created idols that can bring them neither benefit nor harm. Yet, they possess no power over death, nor life, nor resurrection. This state is a clear demonstration that they cannot be deities.\n\nThe disbelievers claimed that the Quran was not revealed by Allah, that the Prophet fabricated it, and that he received assistance from others. Furthermore, they said, 'These are the legends of the ancients; he has had them written down, and they are read to him morning and evening.' Thereupon Allah responds by declaring: 'He who knows the secret in the heavens and the earth has sent it down.' Truly, He is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful; which is why He does not immediately inflict punishment upon the disbelievers.\n\nThe Prophet's eating food and walking in the markets as a human being was criticized by the disbelievers. They said, 'What kind of messenger is this? Would it not have been better if an angel had been sent down to us, or if a treasure had been given to him, or if he had a garden?' Allah, however, proclaims: 'Exalted is He who, if He willed, could grant you better than that—gardens beneath which rivers flow, and palaces.' Yet the disbelievers also denied the Hour; and Allah has prepared a blazing fire for them.\n\nIn the Hereafter, when the disbelievers see the fire of Hell from afar, they will hear its raging and roaring. When they are cast into a narrow space with their hands bound to their necks, they will plead for annihilation not once, but repeatedly. They are told: 'Do not plead today for a single destruction, but plead for many destructions!' As for Paradise promised to the righteous (the God-conscious, muttaqun), it is a reward where they will abide eternally and find whatever they desire. This is a binding promise from Allah that shall be fulfilled.\n\nOn that Day, Allah will gather together those they worshipped besides Him and those who worshipped them, and He will ask the worshipped beings: 'Did you lead these servants of Mine astray, or did they themselves lose the way?' This is an interrogation that exposes the futility of idolatry and the deviance of the disbelievers.\n\nThe polytheists say: 'Glory be to You! It was not proper for us to take protectors besides You; but You bestowed upon them and their forefathers such bounties that in the end they forgot the Remembrance and became a ruined people.' Thereupon they are told that the ones they worshipped have called them liars, and that they can neither avert the punishment nor find help. Allah sent every prophet as a human being who ate food and walked in the markets, and He tested people through one another. The outcome of those who persevere in patience rests upon their Lord being All-Seeing of everything.\n\nIf Allah wills, He could grant the Prophet gardens and palaces in this world as well, but the disbelievers deny the Hour. The particle 'bal' here indicates idrab (digression to a new assertion); that is, they actually know the Quran is not fabricated, but they deny it because they do not believe in the Hereafter. A fierce fire has been prepared for those who deny the Hour. When Hell sees them from a distance, it boils with rage and emits a roar; this represents an attribution of perception to the Fire. In some narrations, it is reported that Hell has eyes and is depicted in this manner on the Day of Resurrection.\n\nOn that Day, the wrongdoer will bite his hands, saying, 'Woe to me! If only I had taken a path with the Messenger; if only I had never taken so-and-so as a friend! He led me astray from the Remembrance (the Quran) after it had come to me.' Satan leaves man utterly forsaken. And the Prophet laments, 'O my Lord! Indeed, my people have treated this Quran as an abandoned thing.' Enemies from among the wicked have been appointed for every prophet, but your Lord is sufficient as a guide and helper.\n\nThe disbelievers demand that the Quran be revealed all at once. Allah, however, aims by revealing it piecemeal to firmly anchor it in the Prophet's heart and to grant him strength in struggle with every newly revealed verse. Moreover, against every argument they bring, He provides the truth and the best explanation. Those who will be gathered face down into Hell have the worst dwelling and the most astray path.\n\nAllah gave Moses (Musa) the Scripture, appointed his brother Aaron (Harun) as a helper, and utterly destroyed the people who denied His verses. When the people of Noah denied the messengers, they were drowned and made a warning sign for humanity. 'Ad, Thamud, the people of al-Rass, and many generations between them were also destroyed. There are various reports concerning the people of al-Rass; most state that they were destroyed because they threw their prophet into a well. The polytheists of Mecca have seen the town upon which the people of Lot were destroyed, yet they drew no lesson, because they did not expect resurrection after death.\n\nThey mock the Prophet, saying, 'Is this the one whom Allah has sent?' and remark, 'He almost led us astray from our gods had we not stood firm by them.' When they behold the punishment, they will know who is truly astray. It is impossible for the Prophet to be a guardian over anyone who takes his own whims and desires (hawa) as his god. Most of them neither listen nor understand; they are like livestock—in fact, even further astray.\n\nAfter describing the deviance of those who worship their own desires and impulses, Allah directs attention to the evidences of His Lordship. It is explained how the shadow is lengthened, that had He willed He could have made it stationary, and then how the sun was made an indicator for the shadow. The shadow is a blessing between light and darkness, symbolizing Allah's protection. Exegetes have interpreted the shadow in various ways: the morning shadow, the canopy of the sky, the entire cosmos, or divine protection. What is essential is recognizing that it is Allah who extends the shadow and worshipping Him accordingly. The sun, as the counterpart of the shadow, is evidence of its existence; were there no light, the shadow could not be known.\n\nThe night has been made a covering, sleep a rest, and the daytime a time for rising and going about to work. Sleep suspends activity much like death, while waking in the morning resembles resurrection. This is a sign pointing to resurrection after death. The winds are sent as harbingers of glad tidings before His mercy, and pure water is sent down from the sky. Through that water, life is restored to dead soil, and drink is provided for livestock and humans. Allah recounts these blessings in various ways so that people may take heed, yet most persist in ingratitude.\n\nIf Allah had willed, He would have sent a warner prophet to every town; however, He sent the Prophet alone. Therefore, one must not yield to the disbelievers and must strive against them in a great jihad with the Quran. This jihad is not a combat fought with one's life, but a struggle waged through the proofs and evidences of the Quran. Then, the fact that two seas—one sweet and the other salty—remain without mixing with each other is a sign of Allah's power. Humankind was also created from water and related through the bonds of lineage and marriage.\n\nDespite all these proofs, the deniers worship things besides Allah that can neither benefit nor harm them. The Prophet is only a bringer of glad tidings and a warner; he asks for no reward for conveying his message, desiring only that people turn toward Allah. The believers must rely upon Allah, the Ever-Living Who does not die, and glorify Him with praise. Allah is fully aware of the sins of His servants; this knowledge is sufficient for punishment.\n\nAllah created the heavens and the earth in six days, then established Himself over the Throne (Arsh). He is the Most Merciful (al-Rahman); He knows all things. When the polytheists are told, 'Prostrate before the Most Merciful,' they deny it, saying, 'And what is the Most Merciful?' and this command only increases their aversion. It is then that the qualities of the true servants of the Most Merciful are described: They walk humbly upon the earth, and when the ignorant address them with insolence, they say, 'Peace' and pass on. They spend their nights in prostration and standing before their Lord.\n\nThe servants of the Most Merciful pray to be saved from the punishment of Hell, for indeed it is an evil settlement and resting place. In their spending, they are neither extravagant nor miserly, but maintain a middle course. They do not associate partners with Allah, nor do they take a life without just cause, nor do they commit adultery. Whoever does these shall meet with multiplied punishment on the Day of Resurrection and abide therein humiliated. But as for those who repent, believe, and do righteous deeds, Allah transforms their evil deeds into good; Allah is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nWhoever repents and acts righteously turns to Allah with an accepted repentance. These servants do not bear false witness, and when they come across vain matters, they pass by with dignity. When reminded of the verses of their Lord, they do not react as if they were deaf and blind. They pray: 'Our Lord! Grant us from among our spouses and offspring comfort to our eyes and make us leaders for the righteous.' Such are those who will be rewarded with the highest stations of Paradise for their patience, welcomed therein with greetings of peace, and abide there eternally.\n\nAt the end of the surah, the Prophet is commanded to say: 'What value would my Lord attach to you if not for your supplication?' The deniers rejected what Allah revealed; therefore, the punishment will inevitably cling to them.\n\nAllah, the Most Merciful, created constellations in the sky. These constellations (buruj) are towering mansions or star clusters. Their number and order demonstrate the power of Allah. Moreover, He placed within them the sun as a radiant lamp and an illuminating moon. It is also He Who made the night and the day follow each other in succession. This alteration is for those who wish to take heed or offer thanks.\n\nThe servants of the Most Merciful walk humbly upon the earth. When the ignorant accost them with words, they say 'Peace' and do not engage with them. They spend their nights prostrating and standing before their Lord, that is, performing prayer. They constantly pray to be saved from the punishment of Hell, for Hell is indeed an evil settlement and resting place.\n\nIn their spending, these servants are neither extravagant nor miserly, but hold to the exact middle course. They do not associate partners with Allah, take a life without just cause, or commit adultery. These are major sins, and their punishment is severe. However, Allah replaces the sins of those who repent and believe with good deeds; He is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nThey do not bear false witness, and when they encounter vain talk, they pass by with dignity. When the verses of their Lord are reminded to them, they do not react as if deaf and blind; rather, they listen with understanding. Furthermore, they pray: 'Our Lord! Grant us spouses and offspring who will be the comfort of our eyes, and make us leaders for the righteous.'\n\nThose who possess these attributes will be rewarded with the highest chambers in Paradise for their patience. There they will be welcomed with greetings of well-being and peace. Say: 'What significance would my Lord attach to you were it not for your supplication? You have denied (the divine message); hence, the punishment for your denial will inevitably be bound to you.'"